issue=f'''<!-- L3S2 ISSUE START --><article class="card build-notice" id="brakeselection-twist"><p class="eyebrow">3L.3-S2 · NEW FOUR-CORNER TRUCK EQUILIBRIUM · 19 OCTOBER 2026</p><h2>Springs can stay compressed while a wheel unloads.</h2><p>This fills a specific mechanics gap: priorS0covered uniform heave;S1covered axle stress, guide tilt and bearings. The new engine solves truck heave/pitch/roll, optional frame warp, each spring force and each axle’s two vertical wheel reactions. It does <strong>not</strong> create new mount geometry or complete the custom truck.</p><p><strong>All displayed numerical fixtures are deliberately synthetic, not project hardware or permitted track defects.</strong> Actual wheel-contact/spring spacing, mass distribution, stiffness, sidebearer law and acceptance limits remain unselected.36in gauge is not substituted for a contact or spring span.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="assets/truck-twist-l3s2/equilibrium.py">Editable equilibrium engine</a><a class="button" href="assets/truck-twist-l3s2/synthetic-fixtures.json">Exact synthetic inputs</a><a class="button" href="assets/truck-twist-l3s2/calculation-results.json">Every force / displacement / balance</a><a class="button" href="assets/truck-twist-l3s2/method-assumptions-sources.json">Derivation / units / limitations</a></div></article><article class="card" id="brakeselection-twist-method"><h2>The rail-to-seat-to-wheel load path</h2><ol><li>At each rigid equal-radius axle, railmean/crosslevel defines axle elevation. Interpolate/extrapolate to each actual lower spring-seat ycoordinate: u=(hR+hL)/2+y_s(hR−hL)/(2b_w).</li><li>Solve upper truck-seat elevation z=h+xθ+yφ+s·w and compression force F=k(u−z), including explicit body reaction and sprung truck weight. Roll restraint is an imposed boundary, not a solved whole-coach response.</li><li>Recover the two wheel reactions from each axle’s force AND moment balance: Q_R+Q_L=F_R+F_L+W_u; b_w(Q_R−Q_L)=b_s(F_R−F_L)+W_u·y_u+M_u.</li><li>Check truck/global and each-axle force/moment residuals independently. Any nonpositive spring force or vertical wheel reaction puts the case outside assumed contact; retain the diagnostic, do not clamp it and pretend a wheel-lift solution.</li></ol><p>b_s and b_w are spring and wheel-contact halfspans. If b_s/b_w=1.5 in a <em>synthetic</em> symmetric axle, diagonal spring-force change is amplified1.5times at the wheel. For a fixed rail-crosslevel difference, the rigid-frame wheel-load change includes a(b_s/b_w)²factor. This is why spring placement and axle leverage must be coordinated before mount forces are assigned.</p><details><summary>Matrix and sign convention</summary><p>Use normalized unknowns q=[h,aθ,b_sφ,w], all in metres. B_i=[1,x_i/a,y_i/b_s,s_i]; omitw for rigid frame. With C=diag(0,0,K_roll/b_s²,K_warp), solve(BᵀKB+C)q=BᵀKu−f+Cq_ref. Downward force moments enter f positively; pitch/roll are elevation slopes. The saved engine and derivation show units and complete balances.</p><p>Negative flatheave is a mathematical compression datum, not installed spring height or floor elevation. Physical free length/preload/seat geometry remains a separate unresolved design.</p></details></article><article class="card" id="brakeselection-twist-cases"><h2>Independent analytical fixtures</h2><p>Base fixture: aggregate corner rate100,000N/m, axle halfspacing1m, spring halfspan0.75m, rail-contact halfspan0.5m, body reaction30kN plus sprung truck weight10kN, and unsprung4kN per axle. These are validation constants, not a proposed36in truck. Baseline F=10kN/corner and Q=12kN/wheel.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Synthetic case</th><th>Spring min / max kN</th><th>Wheel vertical min / max kN</th><th>Model validity only</th></tr></thead><tbody>{fixture_rows}</tbody></table></div><p>The contact-loss diagnostic uses1,000,000N/m rates and9mm lower-seat warp: each spring remains positive at1/19kN, but the linear wheel reactions reach−1.5/25.5kN. This <strong>does not solve a detached wheel</strong>; it demonstrates failure of the four-contact assumption before spring unloading. No real track or vehicle test occurred.</p></article><article class="card" id="brakeselection-twist-compliance"><h2>Frame warp and body roll are different mechanisms</h2><p>For pure lower-seat diagonal warp u_i=s_i·d with equalcornerk: w=4kd/(4k+K_w); ΔF_i=s_i·k·K_w/(4k+K_w)·d. Define eta=K_w/(4k). K_w has unitsN/m for this specific mode—not conventional torsionalNm/rad.</p><p>Below, synthetic d=20mm. The associated front/rear railcrosslevels are−26.667/+26.667mm, a53.333mm crosslevel difference—not a20mm rail defect and not an allowable twist. All other base inputs remain fixed.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimensionless warp eta</th><th>Frame warp mm</th><th>Minimum spring kN</th><th>Wheel vertical min / max kN</th></tr></thead><tbody>{warp_rows}</tbody></table></div><p>A free-warp mode is only an analytical limit, not a designed equalizer. The rigid case removes the warpDOF. Adding stiff body roll restraint does not reduce the pure diagonal warp result in this symmetric model: sidebearers cannot be assumed to cure twist.</p><p>Conversely, a roll restraint changes the response to a uniform crosslevel plane. The imposed body reference is external: actual coach/other-truck roll, sidebearer gap/contact/friction and CG-height/lateral/wind effects are not solved.</p></article><article class="card"><h2>What this can—and cannot—support</h2><ul>{''.join('<li>'+E(s)+'</li>' for s in limitations)}</ul><p>Body bearing load requires the <em>full</em> truck mass to be removed from the whole-coach balance. This model takes body reaction and sprung truck weight separately, then adds each unsprung axle weight once. Do not infer body-bearing force by subtracting unsprung weight only.</p><p>Physical next steps remain controlled wheel/bearing/axlebox and D1geometry, accepted mass/motion/load cases, guide freedom, frame/support law, coil/damper working envelopes and combined dynamics. This calculation method can now be populated and correlated when those inputs arrive; it is not a spring selection, track tolerance, stability assessment or fabrication drawing.</p><p>Primary context checked19October2026: <a href="https://rosap.ntl.bts.gov/view/dot/11952" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FRA/Volpe wheel-unloading report abstract(1986)</a>. It supports investigating vehicle-dependent twist response. No historical numerical limits or regulatory applicability are imported; the matrix is an independently derived analytical model.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="assets/truck-twist-l3s2/project-input-and-release-gates.json">Exact actual inputs still needed</a><a class="button" href="assets/truck-twist-l3s2/test_equilibrium.py">Analytical / invariant tests</a><a class="button" href="assets/truck-twist-l3s2/build_issue.py">Editable fixture / issue source</a></div><p>3L.3-S2 completes this bounded mechanics issue, not3L or the whole truck. No actual springs/mounts/track condition or physical test is approved. Browser interaction remains unverified after managed preview failure. Next:3L.3-S3 actual sidebearer/gap/support and spring-station model reconciliation before any mounting geometry.</p></article><!-- L3S2 ISSUE END -->'''
